NAME
       mkfs - build a file system on a disk or a partition

SYNOPSIS
       **mkfs** __FSTYPE__ __DEVICE__
       **mkfs** __FSTYPE__ __PARTITION__
       **mkfs** **--list**|**-l**
       **mkfs** **--help**|**-h**

DESCRIPTION
       **mkfs** creates a new, empty file system. Everything that was stored
       in the target area is lost; there is no undo.

       The target is either a whole disk or one partition of a disk:

       __DEVICE__
           a disk name as shown by **mkfs --list** or **fdisk -l**, for
           example **sdb**. The file system starts at sector 0 and covers
           the whole disk. Any partition table on that disk is destroyed.

       __PARTITION__
           a disk name followed by the GPT entry number, for example
           **sdb2** for the second entry of the GPT on **sdb**. Only that
           partition is written; the partition table and the other
           partitions stay untouched. Create partitions with **fdisk**(8).

       A **/dev/** prefix is accepted and ignored, so **mkfs ext2 /dev/sdb2**
       works as well.

FILE SYSTEMS
       **ext2**
           The native PureC OS file system. Layout written by mkfs:

             block size          1024 bytes
             blocks per group    8192
             inodes per group    1024
             inode size          128 bytes
             root directory      inode 2, with **.** and **..**

           The volume needs at least 1 MiB (1024 blocks) for a whole disk
           and 128 blocks for a partition. Partitions must end below 2 TiB
           (32-bit sector numbers).

       Other types (fat32, ext3, ext4) are not supported by mkfs yet; the
       FAT32 boot partition is created only by the installer image.

OPTIONS
       **-l**, **--list**
           List the disks with size, model, write mode and transport
           (ATA-PIO, AHCI, USB). The disk that holds the mounted root file
           system is marked **[SYSTEM fs=...]**, offline disks are marked
           **[offline]**. The current root file system type is printed at
           the end.

       **-h**, **--help**
           Show a short usage summary.

SAFETY
       The kernel refuses to format

         - the partition (or the whole disk) that holds the mounted root
           file system;
         - a disk that is read-only or offline;
         - a disk with a sector size other than 512 bytes.

       mkfs confirms the disk by its serial number before the kernel
       writes anything, so a disk that was swapped while mkfs was running
       is not formatted by mistake.

       The kernel writes and flushes the ext2 buffer cache first and
       returns to the root disk afterwards, so formatting a second disk
       does not disturb the running system.

PERMISSIONS
       Formatting needs root (uid 0) or the STORAGE_ADMIN capability.
       **mkfs --list** works for every user.

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success, 1 on any error. Errors are printed with a reason:

         permission denied            not root
         no such device               unknown disk name
         it holds the mounted root    the target is in use
         too small for ext2           below the minimum size
         no GPT partition table       PARTITION given, disk has no GPT
         no such partition            the GPT entry is empty
         beyond 2 TiB                 partition end above 32-bit LBA

EXAMPLES
       Show the disks:

           mkfs --list

       Use a whole second disk as one ext2 volume:

           mkfs ext2 sdb

       Partition a disk and format one partition:

           fdisk sdb          (g, n, w)
           reboot
           mkfs ext2 sdb1

NOTES
       The kernel does not re-read partition tables while running. After
       changing partitions with fdisk, reboot before formatting them.

       After formatting, the new file system is not mounted automatically,
       except on a live system that runs without a root disk.

SEE ALSO
       fdisk(8), df(1), du(1)
